```{r setup, include = FALSE}
# This file is knitted by vignettes/precompute.R (it runs DeepIMC in Python and the standard methods); the package
# ships the knitted interventions.Rmd, so building the package runs no code.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figures/interventions-", dev = "png", dpi = 110,
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, out.width = "100%",
                      cache = TRUE, cache.path = file.path(Sys.getenv("VIGNETTE_CACHE", "cache"), "interventions/"))
options(width = 110)
```

When a restriction is introduced (schools closed, gatherings banned, a
stay-at-home order), the growth of an epidemic curve often changes some days
later. This vignette shows the three steps the package offers to study such a
change, as in the paper:

1. **Where did the curve change?** `split_curve()` finds the breakpoints of
   the growth rate from the counts alone; `find_intervention()` then checks
   whether one of them follows a known restriction within the incubation
   period and the growth falls there (the *intervention cut*).
2. **How did transmission change?** `calibrate_curve()` calibrates the parts
   before and after the cut, each with its own R0 and effective reproduction
   number.
3. **What if the measure had not been taken?** `intervention_effect()` runs
   the fit to the part before the cut on over the part after it, as if
   transmission had not changed, and compares the cases it expects with the
   cases observed: the cases averted.

`vignette("real-curves")` explains splitting, smoothing and recovering a
curve; `vignette("calibrating-windows")` the calibration methods.

```{r packages, cache = FALSE}
library(epiworldRcalibrate)
```

## The data: COVID-19 waves of 2020 and dated measures

`covid_waves_2020` holds the daily COVID-19 cases (by date of report) of 27
waves of 2020 whose restrictions are dated in the Oxford COVID-19 Government
Response Tracker; `interventions` holds those restrictions, and the measures
of the other outbreaks in the package.

```{r data}
data(covid_waves_2020)
data(interventions)
table(covid_waves_2020$wave)
nz <- subset(covid_waves_2020, region == "New Zealand")
nz_measures <- subset(interventions, outbreak == "New Zealand COVID-19")
nz_measures[, c("date", "measure", "kind", "scope")]
```

## 1. Where did the curve change?

Cases by date of report have a weekly cycle, so the split adds day-of-week
effects (`weekday = TRUE`):

```{r split, fig.height = 5.5}
s <- split_curve(nz$cases, nz$date, weekday = TRUE)
s
par(mar = c(7.5, 4, 3, 1))
plot(s, interventions = nz_measures, main = "New Zealand, first wave 2020")
```

The breakpoints come from the counts alone. Only then are they compared with
the known restrictions: a breakpoint is an intervention cut when it comes 0 to
14 days after a restriction (14 days is the longest incubation period of
COVID-19) and the growth rate falls there. Of several, the one with the
largest fall is chosen.

```{r find}
m <- find_intervention(s, interventions, outbreak = "New Zealand COVID-19")
m
```

`max_lag` changes the 14 days, for a disease with a different incubation
period.

## 2. How did transmission change?

We calibrate every part with DeepIMC and the SEIR model, with the known
values of COVID-19 and an effective population of 10,000 (see
`vignette("real-curves")`):

```{r fit}
kv <- known_values("COVID-19", "SEIR")
fit <- calibrate_curve(nz$cases, nz$date, model = "SEIR", method = "DeepIMC", weekday = TRUE, known = kv)
fit
```

R0 describes a population in which everyone is susceptible; `Reff`, R0 times
the share still susceptible on the first day of the part, is the average
number of people one infectious person infects at that time.

```{r recover, fig.height = 5.5}
par(mar = c(7.5, 4, 3, 1))
plot(recover_curve(fit), interventions = nz_measures, main = "New Zealand: each part calibrated by DeepIMC")
```

## 3. The epidemic without the measure

`intervention_effect()` finds the cut with `find_intervention()` (or takes
the one you give with `cut =`), runs the fit to the part before the cut 500
times over both parts, and compares the runs with the counts after the cut:

```{r effect}
eff <- intervention_effect(fit, interventions = interventions, outbreak = "New Zealand COVID-19")
eff
```

```{r effect-plot, fig.height = 5.5}
par(mar = c(5, 4, 4, 1))
plot(eff)
```

The blue bands are each part's own fit; the pink band is the part before the
cut carried on as if nothing had changed. The summary is also a data frame:

```{r effect-summary}
str(eff$summary)
```

## All 27 waves

The same three steps for every wave. As in the paper, only waves with an
intervention cut are calibrated, and a part with more than 10,000 cases (the
effective population) is not calibrated, so the effect cannot be computed
there.

```{r all-waves}
one_wave <- function(r) {
  w <- subset(covid_waves_2020, region == r)
  sp <- split_curve(w$cases, w$date, weekday = TRUE)
  mt <- find_intervention(sp, interventions, outbreak = w$outbreak[1])
  if (!any(mt$chosen)) return(data.frame(region = r, cut = as.Date(NA), note = "no intervention cut"))
  f <- suppressWarnings(calibrate_curve(w$cases, w$date, model = "SEIR", weekday = TRUE, known = kv))
  e <- tryCatch(intervention_effect(f, interventions = interventions, outbreak = w$outbreak[1]),
                error = function(err) conditionMessage(err))
  if (is.character(e)) return(data.frame(region = r, cut = mt$breakpoint[mt$chosen], note = e))
  x <- e$summary
  data.frame(region = r, cut = x$cut, note = "", measure_date = x$measure_date, lag = as.numeric(x$cut - x$measure_date),
             Reff_before = round(x$Reff_before, 2), Reff_after = round(x$Reff_after, 2),
             observed_after = x$observed_after, expected_without = round(x$expected_without),
             averted_pct = round(x$averted_pct))
}
waves <- lapply(unique(covid_waves_2020$region), one_wave)
cols <- unique(unlist(lapply(waves, names)))
waves <- do.call(rbind, lapply(waves, function(z) {
  for (c in setdiff(cols, names(z))) z[[c]] <- if (c == "measure_date") as.Date(NA) else NA
  z[cols]
}))
done <- waves[!is.na(waves$averted_pct), ]
done[order(-done$averted_pct), setdiff(cols, "note")]
```

```{r all-waves-rest}
waves[is.na(waves$averted_pct), c("region", "cut", "note")]
```

Over the `r nrow(done)` waves with an intervention cut that could be
calibrated on both sides, a median of `r round(median(done$averted_pct))`% of
the cases expected without the change did not occur. Where the counts after the
cut come close to the effective population, the fit after the cut describes an
epidemic running out of susceptible people rather than one changed by the
measure, and `print()` of the effect says so.

```{r averted-plot, fig.height = 5.5}
done <- done[order(done$averted_pct), ]
op <- par(mar = c(4, 9, 1, 1), las = 1)
barplot(done$averted_pct, names.arg = done$region, horiz = TRUE, col = "#2a78d6", border = NA,
        xlab = "cases averted after the cut (%)", cex.names = 0.8)
abline(v = median(done$averted_pct), lty = 2)
par(op)
```

## Comparing the methods

The cases averted depend on the fit to the part before the cut. With the
standard methods (minutes per part instead of a fraction of a second):

```{r methods}
methods <- c("DeepIMC", "ABC_SMC", "ABC_MCMC", "DE", "NELDERMEAD")
by_method <- lapply(setNames(methods, methods), function(mm) {
  f <- calibrate_curve(nz$cases, nz$date, model = "SEIR", method = mm, weekday = TRUE, known = kv)
  intervention_effect(f, interventions = interventions, outbreak = "New Zealand COVID-19")
})
tab <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(by_method), function(mm) {
  x <- by_method[[mm]]$summary
  data.frame(method = mm, R0_before = x$R0_before, R0_after = x$R0_after, Reff_before = x$Reff_before,
             Reff_after = x$Reff_after, expected_without = round(x$expected_without),
             expected_lower = round(x$expected_lower), expected_upper = round(x$expected_upper),
             averted_pct = round(x$averted_pct))
}))
tab[, 2:5] <- round(tab[, 2:5], 2)
tab
```

```{r methods-plot, fig.height = 9}
op <- par(mfrow = c(3, 2), mar = c(3, 4, 4, 1))
for (mm in names(by_method)) plot(by_method[[mm]], main = mm)
par(op)
```

## Choosing the cut yourself

The cut need not come from the segmented regression. To cut at a date known
without looking at the counts, for example the day of the measure plus the
mean incubation period (5 days for COVID-19), give it as `split` and `cut`:

```{r own-cut}
measure_day <- m$measure_date[m$chosen]
own <- measure_day + 5
fit_own <- calibrate_curve(nz$cases, nz$date, model = "SEIR", split = own, known = kv)
fit_own
intervention_effect(fit_own, cut = own, interventions = interventions, outbreak = "New Zealand COVID-19")
```

## Another outbreak: Hong Kong SARS 2003

The functions work for any curve with dated measures, for example SARS in
Hong Kong (cases by date of onset, so `weekday = FALSE`):

```{r sars, fig.height = 5.5}
hk <- subset(interventions, outbreak == "Hong Kong SARS 2003")
fit_hk <- calibrate_curve(sars_hongkong_2003$cases, sars_hongkong_2003$date, model = "SEIR",
                          known = known_values("SARS", "SEIR"))
find_intervention(fit_hk, hk)
eff_hk <- tryCatch(intervention_effect(fit_hk, interventions = hk), error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
eff_hk
if (inherits(eff_hk, "intervention_effect")) { par(mar = c(5, 4, 4, 1)); plot(eff_hk) }
```

## What the numbers mean

* The breakpoints are found from the counts alone, and a measure is linked to
  a breakpoint only by its timing. That a breakpoint follows a measure does
  not prove that the measure caused it: other things change at the same time
  (people's own behaviour, testing, the weather). Comparing with places
  without the measure (for example a difference-in-differences comparison, as
  the paper does for Utah's 2020 mask mandate) is needed for that.
* The epidemic without the measure is a scenario of the model, not a
  forecast: it assumes that nothing but the measure changed at the cut.
* Each part is read as the epidemic of an effective population of `n`
  people. When the counts after the cut approach `n`, the fall after the cut
  can come from fewer susceptible people rather than from the measure; when
  the counts before the cut are few, the runs of the part before the cut may
  die out.

